  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The predictive validity of the cycling scale could not be evaluated due to the extremely low prevalence of cycling to school among the study participants.",
      "what": "This study evaluated the reliability and predictive validity of two single-item scales measuring behavioral readiness for active school travel (SC-W for walking and SC-C for cycling) using a sample of 372 Spanish students aged 13.1 ± 2.4 years. Over a 14-day test-retest interval, both scales demonstrated moderate to substantial reliability with weighted kappa values of 0.558 for walking and 0.552 for cycling. Logistic regression models confirmed predictive validity for walking among adolescents (OR = 2.71, p < 0.001), but the association was weaker and not statistically significant for younger children.",
      "why": "The study validates a low-burden, single-item diagnostic tool that researchers and planners can use to assess and segment youth readiness for active travel interventions, showing that these stage-of-change measures are highly predictive for adolescents but may require refinement for younger children."
    }
  },
